    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d99">

                <pb facs="0148" n="74" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg74"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.51/148</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor for the Department of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Polk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of
                        the Treasury</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crosby</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-05-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 23, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Crosby</hi>: In reply to your letter of May 17, I beg to
                    advise you that the telegram enclosed therewith relative to the establishment in
                    the United States of a Russian credit, has been forwarded to the American
                    Ambassador at Petrograd.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d99-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>The expression on page 3 of the telegram, referred to in the second paragraph of
                    your letter, namely, “and our Allies,” was changed to read “and the Allies.”</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
